{"name":"Dolomite Red","hex":"#c5769b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dolomite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evaporite consisting of a mixed calcium and magnesium carbonate, with the chemical formula CaMg(CO3)2; it also exists as the rock dolostone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dolomite"},{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dolomite (rock)","blocks":[{"text":"Dolomite (also known as dolomite rock, dolostone or dolomitic rock) is a sedimentary carbonate rock that contains a high percentage of the mineral dolomite, CaMg(CO3)2. It occurs widely, often in association with limestone and evaporites, though it is less abundant than limestone and rare in Cenozoic rock beds (beds less than about 66 million years in age). One of the first geologists to distinguish dolomite from limestone was Déodat Gratet de Dolomieu, a French mineralogist and geologist after whom it is named. He recognized and described the distinct characteristics of dolomite in the late 18th century, differentiating it from limestone."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/7092_pieskovna_Dolinka_pri_Hradisti_pod_Vratnom_dolomit.JPG/330px-7092_pieskovna_Dolinka_pri_Hradisti_pod_Vratnom_dolomit.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolomite_(rock)"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg/500px-Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg/1280px-Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg/500px-Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Erosion of dolomite over weaker shale created the Niagara Escarpment","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACanadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Sthenarocalymene_celebra_-_Arthropoda%2C_Trilobita%2C_Polymerida%2C_Calymenidae_-_Silurian_-_Ohio%2